Metrological Traceability — Possolo, Bruce, and Watters (@met-traceability: 0, is #reference, #part-of @met-sources) { r_09SQS5GXTSFRF0VSS3X1EEW7WD
Antonio Possolo, Sally S. Bruce, and Robert L. Watters, Jr. Metrological Traceability: Frequently Asked Questions and NIST Policy. NIST Technical Note 2156. [Complete official text](https://www.nist.gov/metrology/metrological-traceability).

Read the complete technical discussion and checklist, especially the distinction between traceability of a result and the histories of an instrument or specimen. Compare the report’s account of NIST’s services with the general VIM definition and the CIPM MRA references. Its policy describes NIST’s role; it is not a universal substitute for every institution’s requirements.

NIST-authored US federal material is generally public domain in the United States, with attribution and separate component notices preserved. The official text, authors, report identity, and scope were checked on 28 October 2026. Use the named report and record any later revision when consulting it. External reference only.

